# Synopsis

Search files under a directory for lines matching regular expressions.
Reading is read-only and needs no approval.

# Patterns

"pattern" is a regular expression, or several regular expressions
separated by newlines; a line matches when it matches any of them. The
syntax is that of ripgrep (the Rust regular expression syntax, close to
extended regular expressions) when rg is available, and extended regular
expressions otherwise. Set "fixed_string" to treat the pattern as a
literal string instead, and "ignore_case" for case-insensitive matching.

# Files

"path" is the directory to search (the working directory by default).
"include" restricts the search to files matching one or more glob
patterns; the glob semantics are those of the glob tool (POSIX pathname
expansion plus the recursive "**" extension), including how hidden files
are handled. Without "include", every file under "path" is searched,
except dot-prefixed (hidden) files.

# Result

The result lists the matching lines grouped by file. The first line
reports the number of lines shown and the total number of matches:

    <count> of <total> matches listed:
    <file>:
      <n>: <content>
      <n>: <content>

    <file>:
      ...

"limit" (default 100) caps the number of matching lines shown: "total"
is the number of all matching lines before the cap, and "count" the
number shown. "n" is the 1-based line number, and "content" the whole
matched line. A path containing control characters is shown
shell-quoted in the `$'...'` (dollar single quote) form.

# JSON format

Set the "output_json" argument to true to return a JSON object instead:

    {"total": <int>, "count": <int>, "matches": [
      {"file": <string>, "lines": [{"n": <int>, "content": <string>}, ...]},
      ...
    ]}

"total" is the number of all matching lines, "count" the number of lines
shown (after "limit"), and "matches" the matches grouped by file, each
with 1-based line numbers "n" and the matched "content".

A "bad argument" error (the argument is not valid JSON) is always
reported in the text format, because "output_json" cannot be read from
a malformed argument.

# Notes

- "limit" must be non-negative.
- "content" is the whole matched line.
